That is a great idea. Refactoring code you already understand is the best way to learn a new pattern.
We are going to take your Job Application Project and upgrade it to use DTOs.
🔄 The New Data Flow
Currently, your API exposes the database directly.
 * Old Way: JSON -> Entity -> Database
 * New Way (The DTO Pattern): JSON -> RequestDTO -> [Conversion] -> Entity -> Database -> Entity -> [Conversion] -> ResponseDTO -> JSON
Here is how to insert DTOs into your existing project, step-by-step.
1️⃣ Step 1: Create the DTO Package
Create a new package: org.example.demo1.dto.
Inside, create two simple classes.
A. The Request (What the user sends)
 * Notice: No ID here. The user cannot fake an ID anymore.
<!-- end list -->
package org.example.demo1.dto;

import lombok.Data;

@Data
public class JobApplicationRequest {
    // Input only needs these 5 fields
    private String name;
    private String email;
    private int experience;
    private double expectedSalary;
    private String techStack;
}

B. The Response (What you send back)
 * This looks similar, but it includes the ID (because the DB generated it) and maybe a success message.
<!-- end list -->
package org.example.demo1.dto;

import lombok.Data;

@Data
public class JobApplicationResponse {
    private Long id;
    private String name;
    private String status; // We will add a nice message here!
}

2️⃣ Step 2: Refactor the Service (The Mapper)
This is where the hard work happens. You must convert the DTO to the Entity before saving, and convert it back after saving.
Change your Interface first:
// Logic: Takes Request DTO, Returns Response DTO
JobApplicationResponse createEntry(JobApplicationRequest request);

Now, update the Implementation:
@Service
public class JobApplicationSerImp implements IJobApplicationService {

    @Autowired
    private JobApplicationRepo jobApplicationRepo;

    @Override
    public JobApplicationResponse createEntry(JobApplicationRequest request) {

        // --- PHASE 1: Validation (Logic Check on DTO) ---
        // (Your existing logic stays mostly the same, just use 'request.get...')
        
        if(jobApplicationRepo.findByEmail(request.getEmail()).isPresent()) {
            throw new DuplicateApplicationException("You have already applied!");
        }

        if(!request.getTechStack().toUpperCase().contains("JAVA")) {
            throw new SkillMismatchException("We only hire Java developers.");
        }
        
        // --- PHASE 2: Map DTO -> Entity (Manual Mapping) ---
        // We create a fresh Entity because the DB needs an Entity, not a DTO.
        JobApplicationModel entity = new JobApplicationModel();
        entity.setName(request.getName());
        entity.setEmail(request.getEmail());
        entity.setExperience(request.getExperience());
        entity.setExpectedSalary(request.getExpectedSalary());
        entity.setTechStack(request.getTechStack());

        // --- PHASE 3: Save to DB ---
        JobApplicationModel savedEntity = jobApplicationRepo.save(entity);

        // --- PHASE 4: Map Entity -> Response DTO ---
        // We create the response object to send back to the user.
        JobApplicationResponse response = new JobApplicationResponse();
        response.setId(savedEntity.getId()); // Get the ID from DB
        response.setName(savedEntity.getName());
        response.setStatus("Application Submitted Successfully"); // Custom message

        return response;
    }
}

3️⃣ Step 3: Refactor the Controller
Your Controller now speaks "DTO Language", not "Entity Language".
@RestController
public class JobApplicationController {

    @Autowired
    private IJobApplicationService jobApplicationService;

    @PostMapping("/")
    // 1. Accept RequestDTO
    public ResponseEntity<?> createEntry(@RequestBody JobApplicationRequest requestDto) {
        try {
            // 2. Call Service (which now returns a ResponseDTO)
            JobApplicationResponse responseDto = jobApplicationService.createEntry(requestDto);

            // 3. Return ResponseDTO
            return new ResponseEntity<>(responseDto, HttpStatus.CREATED);

        } catch (DuplicateApplicationException e) {
            return new ResponseEntity<>(e.getMessage(), HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return new ResponseEntity<>(e.getMessage(), HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
        }
    }
}

🧠 Why did we do this?
 * Safety: The User can never accidentally overwrite the id because JobApplicationRequest simply doesn't have an id field.
 * Cleanliness: Your Response DTO (id, name, status) hides the sensitive data (like expectedSalary or experience) from the final output. You only confirmed what was necessary.
